        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(ports): ignore malformed lsof listener pids",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes a listener-diagnostics bug where malformed `lsof -F` `p` records could still attach subsequent `n` address lines to a listener entry with no valid PID. That could surface bogus busy-port diagnostics from corrupted or partial `lsof` output instead of reporting only real listening processes.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n`src/infra/ports-inspect.ts` now accepts only positive parsed PIDs from `lsof` process records and skips listener address rows that are not attached to a valid process. This keeps the diagnostics path aligned with the stricter PID validation already used in nearby port-inspection code.\n\n## User Impact\n\nGateway port diagnostics now ignore malformed `lsof` process rows instead of showing phantom listeners with missing or invalid process identity. Valid listener output is unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Unit Tests\nFresh focused test runs on commit 16369d50 (after the latest manual `main` merge into this branch):\n\n```bash\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/ports.test.ts src/cli/program.force.test.ts\n[test] starting test/vitest/vitest.infra.config.ts\n\n RUN  v4.1.8 /home/0668000995/10288592/git-hub/test/openclaw\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  16 passed (16)\n   Start at  09:35:28\n   Duration  1.17s (transform 700ms, setup 604ms, import 29ms, tests 301ms, environment 0ms)\n\n[test] starting test/vitest/vitest.cli.config.ts\n\n RUN  v4.1.8 /home/0668000995/10288592/git-hub/test/openclaw\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  22 passed (22)\n   Start at  09:35:35\n   Duration  1.17s (transform 678ms, setup 491ms, import 192ms, tests 161ms, environment 0ms)\n\n[test] passed 2 Vitest shards in 19.99s\n```\n\nCoverage called out by Codex is present on the current branch head:\n- `src/infra/ports.test.ts` keeps the repeated-listener dedupe test\n- `src/infra/ports.test.ts` rejects malformed diagnostics tokens like `p111abc`\n- `src/cli/program.force.test.ts` rejects malformed force-free tokens like `p111abc`\n\n### Real Behavior Proof (for ClawSweeper/Codex)\n\nThe earlier review correctly flagged that mocked Vitest output alone was not enough. Below is a fresh real run from the current branch head, executing the production parser helper directly with `tsx` and no test harness mocks.\n\n```bash\n$ node --import tsx ./tmp-pid-proof.mjs\n=== Real production code execution (no mocks) ===\nLegacy behavior:  Number.parseInt(\"111abc\", 10) = 111\nFixed behavior:   parseStrictPositiveInteger(\"111abc\") = undefined\n\nLegacy behavior would have accepted pid 111 from a corrupted lsof \"p\" record.\nFixed behavior correctly returns undefined so the record is dropped.\n```\n\nThe code path now used by diagnostics is `src/infra/ports-inspect.ts:53-56`:\n\n```typescript\nif (line.startsWith(\"p\")) {\n  const pid = parseStrictPositiveInteger(line.slice(1));\n  processFields = pid !== undefined ? { pid } : {};\n}\n```\n\nThe sibling force-free parser is hardened the same way in `src/cli/ports.ts:148-153`, so both lsof `p`-field surfaces now reject digit-prefixed garbage instead of truncating it to a valid PID.\n\n**Result:** a malformed token like `p111abc` no longer becomes pid `111` on either path. Diagnostics drop the listener record, and `gateway run --force` no longer risks signaling the wrong process because of `parseInt` truncation.\n\n**Current coverage summary:**\n- Valid PIDs (positive integers) → accepted\n- Empty/missing pid record (`p`) → skipped\n- Garbage token (`pabc`) → skipped\n- Trailing-garbage token (`p111abc`) → skipped on both diagnostics and force-free paths\n- Repeated identical listener records for one process/address → deduplicated\n- Address rows without a valid PID context → ignored\n\n\n",
        "branch": "fix/ports-inspect-invalid-lsof-pids",
